# Pages where one engine put ink the other put none near, each with the
# reason. An entry is a basename and " pN", as nopage's are, and it is
# removed the day its reason stops holding.
#
# Two correct renderings of a page of text differ by thousands of
# pixels, because one engine anti-aliases its glyphs and the other does
# not. The evaluator therefore counts something else as well: ink one
# engine marked solidly with no mark of any strength from the other
# within two pixels of it. Anti-aliasing cannot produce any of that, and
# neither can a half-pixel of stem placement; what can is a mark in a
# place the other engine left blank, which is either something that
# moved, something that was drawn and should not have been, or two
# renderings that were never of the same page.
#
# So an entry here is a difference nobody can attribute to the
# rasterisers, and every one of them has to be attributed to something
# else in writing. The file is read both ways every run: a page that
# displaces ink and is not named here fails the corpus, and so does a
# page named here that no longer displaces any. The second direction is
# the one that keeps the file true -- a reason nothing measures again
# goes on being read as a known cost long after it has stopped being
# one.
#
# The evaluator reports the number for every page and names only the
# ones at or above a floor, which is thirty pixels: below it the two
# rasterisers are disagreeing about the edge of a glyph, which they do a
# pixel at a time and in single figures over a whole page, and above it
# the ink of something the size of a character has moved. A character of
# ten-point text at seventy-two dots to the inch marks about thirty
# pixels and a character that moved is counted twice, once where it went
# and once where it no longer is, so the floor is half of the smallest
# thing this corpus can move. Two pages sit under it today, gb_04-01 at
# seven pixels and gb_04-02 at eight, and both are the dot of an i
# placed a row apart by the two glyph rasterisers.
#
# Nothing here is xpost declining to draw something. A page this
# interpreter draws blank or draws wrongly is a defect and belongs in a
# commit, not in this file.
#
# gb_07-04-listing p1, gb_07-05-listing p1, gb_08-02-listing p1 --
#           three listings from the chapters on font selection and
#           re-encoding, and all three set text in StoneSerif or
#           StoneSerif-Italic, which is a typeface neither engine has.
#           Each substitutes what it has, and they do not choose the
#           same face: the two pages then carry the same words in
#           different letterforms at different widths, and past the
#           first few characters of a line nothing sits where the other
#           engine put it. gb_08-02 sets a single 300-point word, so
#           almost the whole of its ink is displaced; the other two set
#           a line and a paragraph at fifty and ninety point. This is a
#           property of what the two engines were installed with rather
#           than of either of them, and no comparison of these pages
#           says anything about the renderer until the typeface is
#           present on both sides. gb_06-01 asks for a font by name in
#           the same way and does not appear here: its substitution
#           happens to land close enough that no ink is displaced,
#           which is luck and not agreement.
#
# gb_10-02-listing p1, gb_10-04-listing p1 -- neither page is compared
#           at all, because the two engines did not draw the same sheet
#           of paper. Both listings ask for ledger paper the way a
#           document of their period does, by testing whether the
#           product dictionary defines a tray for it and calling the
#           tray operator if it does; the alternative branch scales a
#           letter sheet down for proofing instead. The dictionary they
#           test is a product's, not the language's -- PLRM 8.2 gives
#           statusdict no contents of its own and leaves what is in it
#           to the product -- so the two engines take opposite branches
#           by design, and one renders 1224 by 792 while the other
#           renders 612 by 792. The evaluator reports MEDIA for these
#           rather than a difference, because a count of differing
#           pixels reads only the overlap of two such pages and would
#           report gb_10-04 -- which draws no marks at all, being a
#           paper-size selection and a bare showpage -- as a perfect
#           match between two different sheets.
#
#           These go when the corpus can say that a program selects its
#           medium outside the language, which is a thing to declare
#           about the program rather than about the difference it
#           produces: the pages are not comparable, and that is true
#           before either engine is run.
#
gb_07-04-listing p1
gb_07-05-listing p1
gb_08-02-listing p1
gb_10-02-listing p1
gb_10-04-listing p1
